  "body": "<p>A warning at the Refuge becomes a search through dark caverns. A fresh blood painting leads the company to living captives, and an entrusted seed turns rescue into a responsibility that no single victory can finish.</p><h2 id=\"the-warning-at-home\">The warning at home</h2><p>Aerrow was huddled inside Skylight Refuge when the others gathered. Beyond the caves, immense beasts roared. He said he had found the island’s people torn apart and believed the hunters were coming closer to the shelter each day. He feared that the resistance would be found and destroyed. His warning demanded an answer from people whose own losses had already made action difficult.</p><p>Olokun felt the weight of the time they had spent elsewhere. Scribonia admitted that his personal projects were not the whole reason for his absence: repeated terrible deaths had left him afraid of another end. Olokun answered that strength came from having a team behind him. That reassurance would soon be tested underground.</p><p>Gartina arrived anxious about her first expedition in this land. Plans multiplied around her: distract the giants, set controlled fires, manipulate the weather or search for the hidden people. She questioned the damage a fire might do to the cover that kept them alive. Elenia wanted contact with the island’s people. Arjahn thought the people might help them fight, but his own anger was already pulling him toward destruction.</p><h2 id=\"the-plan-becomes-a-trail\">The plan becomes a trail</h2><p>A restored but unresponsive warrior could not settle their questions. Aerrow pointed toward caverns the company already knew, and the discussion grew more strained. Olokun finally ended it by leading everyone there. Arjahn disliked the impulsiveness but would not let him go alone. Gartina followed, frustrated that so much preparation had ended in a different plan.</p><p>The route carried old memories. Olokun warned of petrifying machines and huge serpentine creatures. Arjahn remembered the darkness in which he had once sealed himself away to survive. He did not hide his temptation to raze the place. Scribonia argued that protecting a land could not mean burning everything in it; Elenia questioned what they were going to accomplish.</p><p>At the entrance, blood and tracks gave the company a more immediate purpose. Gartina noticed red behind vines concealing a wall. When Olokun pulled them away, another painting appeared. Someone had hurriedly used fresh blood to show small figures being driven toward the cave mouths. This was not an ancient account of a vanished people. The warning was recent, and someone might still be saved.</p><h2 id=\"darkness-and-torn-cloth\">Darkness and torn cloth</h2><p>The company disagreed even over light. A glowing vine would let them see and cast, but it would also announce their approach. They chose to go darker. Gartina concealed their passage, while Scribonia, who could still see, became their eyes. Arjahn listened and felt along the ground rather than pretend he could see as well as the others.</p><p>At a fork, large passages led one way and smaller tunnels the other. A scrap of bloodied cloth caught in a stalagmite supplied the choice. It was as recent as the blood outside. The group took the smaller passages. Older armed bones lay farther in, resembling the equipment of the warrior at the Refuge; those remains did not make the fresh trail any less urgent.</p><p>Gartina heard the measured sound of metal on stone ahead. Then Elenia knocked a loose rock into the passage. The footsteps stopped. Something had heard them. They pressed against the wall and held their light while the creatures approached. Arjahn traced a warning on Elenia’s hand. The careful search had become an ambush neither side could now avoid.</p><h2 id=\"the-machines-in-the-passage\">The machines in the passage</h2><p>The things crawling toward them were dragon-like shapes of bone and metal. Olokun warned about the tails he remembered petrifying companions before. Elenia brought light and fire into the fight; Scribonia attacked with lightning. Their spells hurt the machines, but the company did not pass through untouched.</p><p>A grinding mechanical scream dazed Gartina and brought Arjahn back to an earlier terror. Scribonia was stunned and caught in a creature’s jaws. Olokun struck at it to defend his friend, while Gartina moved close enough to heal him. Arjahn forced himself back into the fight. The promise that they would rely on one another was becoming practical work.</p><p>Then faint cries reached them from beyond a red back door. The voices were human. Scribonia and Elenia could tell that this was not another false lure: people were trapped there. The company stopped treating the passage as somewhere merely to survive. They healed what they could and moved toward the door.</p><h2 id=\"light-in-the-captive-chamber\">Light in the captive chamber</h2><p>The door was sealed from within, without the magical wards Scribonia checked for. As it broke apart, Olokun found himself facing an executioner. The horror answered their arrival with deadly toxin and claimed that Abyss had expected their foolishness. Frightened living people were still within its reach.</p><p>Elenia called for help. Mya’s light filled the room, freed the captives from their restraints and protected them from the gas and contamination. It did not make them immune to every danger in the chamber, including the rescuers’ own attacks. Elenia told them to move away from the monster.</p><p>Olokun kept the executioner occupied despite terrible blows. Gartina’s attempt to transform it failed; Elenia’s attempt to send it away did not hold. Scribonia looked for a way past its defenses, and Arjahn chose to keep Olokun fighting rather than surrender to his own desire to strike. The captives needed them to do more than express their fury.</p><h2 id=\"the-painter-and-the-seed\">The painter and the seed</h2><p>The executioner’s magical protection finally broke. Its armor shattered, but its form became mist and slipped into the walls. The company had won room to save the people in front of them. They had not established that this enemy was destroyed for good.</p><p>A nomad emerged from behind the pillars and asked Olokun whether he was the painter. The survivors had seen his attempts to communicate. They had not known whether to trust them, because their hunter had used so many tricks. A message made in hope had required a living rescue before it could become trust.</p><p>The nomad then approached Elenia and delivered a great golden seed. He said it was one of four and that word had reached them of an enemy destroying one. The number and destruction report remained his account. The seed in Elenia’s hands was real, and she promised to protect it. Winning a chamber had given her something the island’s future might depend on.</p><h2 id=\"a-way-out-and-work-ahead\">A way out, and work ahead</h2><p>Olokun asked whether the survivors could be taken to the Refuge by magic. Scribonia had spent too much power to Teleport. Arjahn offered what healing remained. For now, they had a quiet, safe route out through the tunnels.</p><p>Gartina brought out food. Younger, timid survivors came forward, their hunger visible in their ribs. Arjahn apologized for the chances the adventurers had missed and called this a beginning of making things right. Their success could not restore the people who had died while they were elsewhere.</p><p>The later community at <a href=\"#skylight-refuge\">Skylight Refuge</a> would give these promises a daily form: food, secrecy, repairs and decisions about whether to stay. Protecting the seed would require more than accepting it. Their promises would be measured in food, shelter and continued protection.</p><p>Continue reading: <a href=\"#defense-of-skylight-refuge\">The Defense of Skylight Refuge</a>.</p>",
